  ORCHESTRA
  =========

    fresh thinking:
      this'll be easier to do if everything has to pass through Trigger.
      Trigger can be like airport customs logging everythign that happens.
      Because everything must be done by one of three kickoff types, the type + Entity can mark some kind of group ID.
      So, while the callback *may* tell the entity to move, Trigger knows subsequent events like this correspond to the last kickoff.
      Therefore, nobody has direct access to anything besides Trigger. ( "I am the way... Nobody comes to the Father except through me." -John 14:6 )
      Individual elements need a key to correspond to a system. Motion, collision, animation, timers, 
      \ How can sub-timers be recognized as such? New boolean.
      Should timers be smart enough to correspond to a system instead of a callback? 
      Group should have priority, as should every collision, input, and timer. Interruption is guaged this way.
      We need to be able to do the following things through Trigger:
        + START (g)roup or (i)ndividual element
        + STOP (g)roup or (i)ndividual element
        + PAUSE (g)roup or (i)ndividual element
        + UNPAUse (g)roup or (i)ndividual element
      
      (i) Elements have IDs, and (g) 0 means all.
      So that way, we can just have two layers of wrappers in lua:

      From Lua to C++:
          self:hide()  ->   stop( RENDER, entity )  ->  send(  STOP, RENDER, entity )
          (T)echnical bridge deets
              
      From C++ to Lua:
          onInput( )
          onTimeout()
          onCollision()
          (T)echnical bridge deets

      With such a limited set, I'm no longer convinced Personality is a necessary structure. That can be done in Lua.
    
  LOADING
  =======
    loading entities from genomes and passing their IDs to lua-side
    hooking scenes together through graph

